    {
      "source": "data/inference.jsonl",
      "input": {
        "question_id": "11384_8353800736210",
        "question": "Based on the chest X-ray findings in Figure 1, what is the most likely characteristic of the identified pulmonary opacities, and how do these findings correlate with Idiopathic Hypereosinophilic Syndrome (IHS)?  \nA) The opacities are well-defined with peripheral ground-glass halos, suggesting an infectious process typically seen in conditions like invasive aspergillosis.  \nB) The opacities are ill-defined with diffuse distribution, consistent with eosinophilic infiltration, which aligns with pulmonary involvement in IHS.  \nC) The opacities are well-circumscribed nodules, indicating a neoplastic process such as bronchioloalveolar carcinoma.  \nD) The opacities are sharply marginated and localized to the lower lobes, consistent with pulmonary embolism.  \nE) The opacities are accompanied by pleural effusion, which is common in IHS, suggesting an advanced stage of the disease.  \nF) The opacities are confluent with lobar distribution, indicative of bacterial pneumonia.",
        "images": [
          "cxr_020.png"
        ]
      }
    },
